<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="ac2ad212">&lt;p&gt;
    A technique that assists with the generation of multidimensional reports.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;Online Analytical Processing (OLAP):&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Historical data from multiple lines of business (LOB) is integrated from various systems in an aggregated form to
    provide a broader landscape of the enterprise. This data contains numerous business categories, which are essential for
    analytical purposes like data filtering and aggregation and hierarchical grouping. These business categories are
    defined as dimensions (Product, LOB, Geography, Channel etc.) and the quantitative values captured around these
    dimensions are called facts. This process is termed as OLAP and the corresponding multivariate data structures
    containing pre-aggregated data are referred to as OLAP cubes.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;strong&gt;OLAP Cubes:&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    OLAP cubes are used to generate predefined reports on a regular basis (canned/package reports) for both internal and
    external audiences for various analytical purposes.&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;br /&gt;
    OLAP cubes have a number of benefits for the end users. Aligning data as a cube and forming hierarchical dimensions
    allows for simple and straightforward analysis in a number of ways such as a) slice and dice of data, b) drill up/drill
    down of data, c) roll-up and pivot of data. In addition, since a cube is pre-summarized across dimensions this
    drastically speeds up the query and output time over relational databases. The MDX query language is typically used to
    interact with cubes.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
